## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` per iteration |
| `GetNextItem()` blocks | Blocking call interruption | Pass token to blocking call |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking can be interrupted
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just let the loop condition exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, there's nothing equivalent to Abort().
        // The thread must cooperate. Log a warning if needed.
        if (_workerThread?.IsAlive == true)
        {
            // In modern .NET you cannot force-kill a thread.
            // If GetNextItem/ProcessItem don't respect the token, 
            // move uncooperative work to a child process.
            Console.WriteLine("Warning: worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### What Must Change in `GetNextItem`

The original could block indefinitely. You **must** make it token-aware:

```csharp
// If using a BlockingCollection<T>:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
}

// If using a manual wait:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Replace: _signal.WaitOne();
    WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _signal, token.WaitHandle });
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return DequeueItem();
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to `GetNextItem`** | If the call blocks forever, cancellation never fires — your thread hangs on `Stop()` |
| **Using `Thread.Sleep` for delays** | Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` so cancellation wakes the thread |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and registered callbacks |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only triggers at the next checkpoint; add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations in `ProcessItem` |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException` broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to only catch *your* cancellation, not unrelated ones |

### The Fundamental Shift

**.NET Framework:** Runtime injects `ThreadAbortException` at arbitrary points → code is interrupted preemptively.

**.NET 8:** Code must **voluntarily check** the token. If `ProcessItem` runs a 30-second computation without checking, cancellation waits 30 seconds. Design checkpoint frequency based on acceptable stop latency.
